n fact suffer and I think maybe  it will be good for us to think in terms  of both in the lives of both its promise  and also a failure and betrayal to think  maybe that for both faith and  globalization it might be good to think  that the effects of both of these two on  the poor and on the weak are one of the  criteria important criteria how we judge  both fades and globalization processes  and I think the background for that  might be a background presupposition  might be that and maybe that's  controversial but that's how I would put  it that a humanity of a system or  humanity of a society is measured by how  it treats it weakest members and if  that's the case that provides us quite  an important a criterion is maybe too  strong and too kind of clearly  formulated but at least the guideline in  terms of how to assess the performance  quote-unquote of those faith traditions  and and economic side of globalization  processes second comment that I want to  make concerns the notion of what it  means to be poor and what it means to be  wealthy that's I think among many other  things that faith traditions contribute  to this whole debate is one of these  things you can put it differently also  you can ask the question now you'll  smile a laugh because you've heard me  ask that question many times what's the  wealth for right I ask the question  what's the whole economic system for but  also what is wealth for put differently  how it fits into the larger account of  what it means to lead one lives once  live well and especially if we  distinguish between safely say that  human flourishing consists of has to  formal components as i said before life  going well for one  and life being led well now we can all  agree that it's hard to argue that life  going well for one that life is going  well for one if they live on one dollar  a day right or even less in many many  cases no access to health and so forth  so the concern for reaching a certain  threshold of of wealth so as to be able  to be nourished to be able to have a per  tunity to thrive all of us would agree  on this in terms of constituting kind of  poetic and a bottom-line absolute almost  poverty but I think it's very important  for for a religious folks from religious  perspective to think about what  constitutes then wealth and what  constitutes poverty above that threshold  and I think one can distinguish in most  of the traditions something like what I  might call the two types of people right  one type of person is not enough person  no matter what you've got and how you  how you fare you're just not enough it's  always needs to be more right and always  more than enough person right now always  more than enough person is really a  funny thing to to to invoke in the in  today right it seems counter to many of  the basic intuitions we have about how  the whole world and wealth creation is  being run but in just about all  religious traditions there is something  like that of more than enough kind of a  person I think that goes that's behind  some of the detachment ideas in Buddhism  in Hinduism that's certainly behind the  idea of contentment whether that's a  stoic version of contentment or that  that's a Christian version of other  religious versions of contentment and it  seemed to me that something of that sort  needs to be there for people actually to  be willing to engage in care for the  poor if you are simply concerned about  yourself and if you see yourself as not  enough person what will take you outside  of yourself in order to reach out  whether that's reaching out in giving  others whether that's reaching out in  creating conditions where they could  thrive whatever  that might be I think poverty  alleviation presume presumes people who  are well just about just slightly more  than enough people at least right Co  able to be able to give engage with a  question and problem of poverty whether  that's a large structural level or very  small personal level I